---
import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro';

const CHECKED_ON = '22 August 2026';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Is Odoo bad software?',
    a: 'No. It is capable software aimed at a different buyer. The difference is what it costs in time and expertise to get from signing up to a working shop, and who does that work.',
  },
  {
    /* The old answer leaned on guided setup ("we can, including loading your
       stock ... optional and priced separately"). That offer went on
       8 October 2026 along with the monthly fee, and the site no longer says
       what the one-time setup fee covers, so this sticks to what the product
       itself asks of a shop. */
    q: 'How long does QuadERP take to set up?',
    a: 'Mostly as long as it takes to get your products in, because there is nothing to configure. Products and opening stock can be imported from a spreadsheet. You pay the one-time setup fee and your first year before you start, and the live demo lets you see the whole thing before you do.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What if I outgrow QuadERP?',
    a: 'Then you export your data and move. That is a real answer rather than a polite one: the export is self-serve and it is your data. A system you cannot leave is a worse purchase than one you can.',
  },
];
---

<PageLayout page="odoo-alternative-ghana" faqs={faqs} faqHeading="Questions people ask when comparing">
    <p>
        Odoo is a large, modular business system that can be configured into almost anything,
        including a very good retail setup. That flexibility is genuine and it is also the whole
        trade-off, because someone has to do the configuring and that someone is not usually the
        person who owns the shop.
    </p>

    <h2>The question is not features, it is who does the work</h2>
    <p>
        A modular system asks you to decide which modules, how they connect, what your workflow
        is, and then to keep those decisions maintained. Businesses that run Odoo well normally
        have an implementation partner and a budget for one. If you have that, it is a serious
        option and we are not going to pretend otherwise.
    </p>
    <p>
        QuadERP has made those decisions already, for one kind of business: a retail shop in
        Ghana with a counter, stock and staff. Fewer choices, and none of them yours to make.
    </p>

    <div class="erp-compare-wrap">
        <table class="erp-compare-table">
            <thead>
                <tr><th></th><th>QuadERP</th><th>A modular ERP</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td>Shape</td><td>One system, retail, opinionated</td><td>Modules you assemble</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Who configures it</td><td>Nothing to configure: it is built for retail</td><td>You, or a partner you pay</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Time to selling</td><td>As soon as your products are in</td><td>Depends entirely on scope</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Fits an unusual process</td><td>Only if retail-shaped</td><td>Yes, that is its strength</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Manufacturing, projects, HR depth</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Priced in</td><td>Cedis</td><td>Varies</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
    <p class="erp-checked-on">
        The QuadERP column is checked against what ships today. The right-hand column describes
        modular ERP as a category rather than one vendor's current price list. Checked {CHECKED_ON}.
    </p>

    <h2>Where Odoo is the better buy</h2>
    <p>
        If you manufacture, run projects, need deep HR, or have a process that is genuinely unlike
        anyone else's, you want something you can shape. QuadERP will feel narrow, because it is.
        It does retail.
    </p>

    <h2>Where this is the better buy</h2>
    <p>
        If you sell things over a counter in one or several shops and what you actually want is to
        stop losing stock and start seeing your numbers, the narrowness is the feature. There is
        nothing to configure, your products come in from a spreadsheet, and the live demo lets you
        and your staff try the whole thing before you are asked for money.
    </p>
    <p>
        See <a href="/pos-system-ghana/">the POS</a>,
        <a href="/inventory-management-software/">stock</a>, and
        <a href="/accounting-software-ghana/">the money side</a>, including what the money side
        does not do.
    </p>
</PageLayout>
